I am estimating a bear population over a large area and we set up a stratified sampling design using trap clusters.  The clusters consist of 5 or so traps about 2000 m apart.  Primary habitat was sampled with clusters 16000 m apart on center and secondary habitat with clusters 25000 m apart on center, meaning that there were fewer clusters in the secondary habitat but the clusters themselves were arranged the same way.  My question is whether I can combine primary and secondary data and analyze as one session.  We had no captures of individuals in more than 1 cluster (no cross-cluster movement).  Since the spacing between traps in a cluster is consistent in both primary and secondary zones, my guess is that density is based wholly on those spacings and the estimates of density in the spaces between clusters should be unbiased.  It would simplify the analysis if I could do it in 1 session but I wanted to check with the group first.  Thanks!  Joe

