Thanks for those insights Eric.  I guess my point is that, since there is no cross-cluster movement, D, g0, and sigma are not estimated less intensively in the secondary habitat because those estimates all come from data collected from within the clusters (not across them), which are the same dimensions in both.  True, there are fewer clusters in the secondary areas, which will increase the SE, but the actual estimates should be unbiased.  I am thinking that habitat covariates on D or maybe a distance parameter from the core population would help account for the lower D in the secondary areas if all are from the same session.

Your thought about detection probabilities is a good one and we certainly will look at habitat covariates for D, g0 and sigma.  It might be that a multi-session analysis is necessary but what I am asking is whether there is any theoretical reason why they should not be pooled.  This is an already complex analysis over a very wide range and I was trying to simplify things if possible.
Joe

